# The machine looking at itself, and this collection looking at
# itself.  Some of these meet os9exec's limits rather than their
# own -- where they do, the screen says so.

shot    signal
cap     Sends an OS-9 signal to a process by id -- the id, the signal
cap     code, and seconds to wait first if you want a delay.  Code 0 ends
cap     the process.  Here `cron' is started in the background and bash
cap     prints the id it got, in angle brackets -- use the id you see
cap     rather than the 4 shown here.  Sent twice: the second answers 228,
cap     an illegal process id, which is how you know the first one landed.
try     signal 4 0
run     set +H
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     cron &
wait    4
run     signal 4 0; echo "status $?"
wait    4
run     signal 4 0; echo "status $?"
wait    4
shot    unc
cap     unc disassembles a 68000 OS-9 module back to assembler: the module
cap     header as equates, then the code, tracing which bytes are
cap     instructions and which are data, and naming the OS-9 system calls.
try     unc setime
run     builtin cd /dd/CMDS
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     unc setime
wait    5

shot    about
size    30 80
cap     The collection's own guide: `about hack' answers what a program is,
cap     where it came from, what it opens and whether its source survived --
cap     out of the four files the web catalogue is built from, read at the
cap     moment you ask, so it cannot drift from what shipped.
try     about hack
run     about hack
wait    5
shot    which
size    24 100
cap     Says what a command name runs, and where it came from.  It looks where
cap     the OS-9 shell looks -- memory, the execution directory, each PATH
cap     directory, then the data directory -- and stops at the first; -a lists
cap     every place.  A module already in memory is traced to the file that
cap     holds the same module, whatever the file is called: ckermit's module
cap     is named wermit.
try     which -a gnuchess
run     which cat
run     which -a gnuchess
run     load ckermit
run     which wermit
wait    6
shot    whereis
size    24 100
cap     Finds a program's source, its command and its documentation.  It
cap     searches SRC, CMDS, DEFS, LIB and DOC all the way down, on this disk and
cap     on every device PATH names, so source filed under the archive it came
cap     in -- gen's is in divutils -- still turns up.  Source is listed first,
cap     then commands, then documentation.
try     whereis gen
run     whereis gen
run     whereis keep tex
wait    6
shot    aprocs
cap     A process monitor: it prints the active processes as a tree -- id,
cap     parent, priority, CPU time, age and share of the CPU -- reading the
cap     ages through F$SetSys's Julian and second-of-day globals.  Here it
cap     catches the shell and itself.
try     aprocs
run     aprocs
wait    5
shot    argproc_demo
cap     argproc_demo parsing a command line, which is the whole point of
cap     it: argproc() takes the switches, fills in the defaults for the
cap     ones you left out, and hands back the filename.  A switch takes
cap     its argument with no space -- `-x99', never `-x 99'.
try     argproc_demo readme; argproc_demo -x99 -sHello readme; argproc_demo -help
run     argproc_demo readme
wait    6
run     argproc_demo -x99 -sHello readme
wait    6
run     argproc_demo -help
wait    8
shot    bigsetter
cap     A Modula-2 set-operations demonstration -- proof the Modula
cap     compiler's output runs here.
try     bigsetter
run     bigsetter
wait    8
shot    bootgen
cap     bootgen builds the boot file a machine starts from -- the modules
cap     it loads before anything else, one after another in one file.
cap     Here it makes a new one, 256 sectors, on a RAM disk from two
cap     modules, wc and head; -h would also hook it into the disk's
cap     identification sector so the machine finds it, which is left alone
cap     here.  The RAM disk was made first with `mount -r=256k /r0'.
try     bootgen -n=256 /r0 wc head
run     mount -r=256k /r0 > /nil 2>/nil
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd/CMDS
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     bootgen -n=256 /r0 wc head
wait    6
run     ls -l /r0
wait    4
shot    clock
size    30 80
cap     A full-screen clock: the day, the date and the time in large
cap     letters, redrawn as the time changes.  It draws each line by running
cap     `banner' through `shell' -- your own OS-9's, which SYS/login loads
cap     from /h1 -- and reading the banner back from a pipe.  Control-C
cap     stops it.
try     clock
run     load /h1/CMDS/shell
wait    3
run     clear
wait    1
run     clock
wait    10
snap
send    \003
wait    2
shot    config
cap     config reports this machine's C types as C comments: the size of
cap     char, short, int and long, their alignments and the pointer size,
cap     then the precision and range of float and double.  Its first
cap     twenty lines are shown.
try     config | head -20
run     config | head -20
wait    5
shot    cpu
cap     A CPU speed test: it draws a bar chart of its timing loop and
cap     prints the clock rate it measured, then stops on a trap and the
cap     session ends with it, which is why the screen stops where it does.
try     cpu
run     cpu
wait    10
shot    dam
cap     The disk allocation map: one character per cluster across a
cap     255-megabyte volume, so you can see where the collection sits on
cap     it.
try     dam /dd | head -n 21
os9     dam /dd !head -n 21
run     dam /dd | head -n 21
wait    10
shot    demo
cap     demo, the egetopt option-parsing example, showing how it read the
cap     command line it was given.
try     demo -a -b -c=7 one two
run     demo -a -b -c=7 one two
wait    6
shot    devprc
for     devprc ptxminst t_trtest
cap     devprc shows which device each process holds a path to: `-a' walks
cap     every process and lists its open paths and their devices.  `ptxminst'
cap     installs Ptxm, and `t_trtest' is a test driver for tree functions.
try     devprc -a; ptxminst -?; t_trtest
run     devprc -a
wait    8
run     ptxminst -?
wait    8
run     t_trtest
wait    8
shot    dinfo
cap     dinfo reports a device's geometry and free space -- here the volume
cap     the collection is mounted as: its size, sector size and how much of
cap     it is in use.
try     dinfo /dd
run     dinfo /dd
wait    5
shot    dload
cap     Loads a data file into a data module in memory, despite the name
cap     -- it does not send anything down a serial line.  Given the
cap     message of the day it reports the size it read and the address
cap     the module was placed at.
try     dload motd
run     builtin cd /dd/SYS
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     clear
run     dload motd
wait    7

shot    unkeep
size    30 80
cap     unkeep takes back what keep wrote.  A program is kept first, then
cap     unkept: unkeep reads the receipt in SYS/kept and removes each file
cap     it lists, leaving any whose checksum has changed since -- a score
cap     file, a saved game -- so those are safe from it.
try     unkeep greg
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/SYSB > /nil 2>/nil; export KEEP_TO=/dd/tmp/SYSB/kp; rm -rf /dd/tmp/SYSB/kp > /nil 2>/nil; keep greg > /nil
wait    5
run     clear
wait    1
run     unkeep greg
wait    5
shot    every
cap     every runs a command again and again at a fixed interval:
cap     `every 2 oskversion' asks the kernel its version every two seconds
cap     until you stop it.  The first two answers are on the screen.
try     every 2 oskversion
run     every 2 oskversion
wait    14
kill
shot    fastcc
size    32 80
for     fastcc lfmaker
cap     fastcc, a quicker front end for cc, and lfmaker, which builds a
cap     G-Windows launch file.  lfmaker prints nothing, bare or given a
cap     file, and returns 208 (E$UnkSvc): it asks the terminal for a
cap     G-Windows service a character terminal does not have.
try     fastcc -?; lfmaker; lfmaker readme
run     fastcc -?
wait    6
run     lfmaker
wait    4
run     lfmaker readme
wait    6
shot    flink
cap     flink makes a second directory entry for a file, under a name you
cap     give -- an RBF hard link.  It is shown by its usage: RBF has no true
cap     hard links, and removing such an entry can leave the original
cap     pointing at the wrong place, so it is not run on the disk here.
try     flink -?
run     flink -?
wait    5
shot    gen
size    30 80
cap     gen writes the frame of a new C program -- header block,
cap     authorship and version lines and sectioned comments.  It appends
cap     `.c' to the name you give, which is why it looks as though it
cap     wrote nothing: `gen -p demo' leaves demo.c, whose head is shown
cap     here.
try     gen -p tmp/SYSB/demo; cat tmp/SYSB/demo.c | head -n 16
os9     gen -p tmp/SYSB/demo; cat tmp/SYSB/demo.c !head -n 16
run     rm -f /dd/tmp/SYSB/demo.c > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/SYSB > /nil 2>/nil
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     gen -p tmp/SYSB/demo
wait    5
run     cat tmp/SYSB/demo.c | head -n 16
wait    6
shot    getsys
size    24 80
cap     getsys reports OS-9's system globals, reading the global page
cap     field by field -- the module directory, the process descriptor
cap     table, the clock, the FPU the system believes it has.  Ninety
cap     lines in all; these are the first twenty.  A dozen or so fields
cap     come back as notices rather than values, and those go to standard
cap     error, which is what the redirect sets aside.
try     getsys 2>/nil | head -n 20
os9     getsys >>>/nil !head -n 20
run     getsys 2>/nil | head -n 20
wait    6
shot    hinterhalt
for     hinterhalt testibc
cap     testibc, a menu for driving an IEEE-488 instrument bus -- send,
cap     receive, clear, remote and local control -- from the same EFFO
cap     forum shelf as hinterhalt.  It asks for a timeout time first;
cap     answered, its menu is what there is to show with no real bus
cap     attached.  hinterhalt is a small maze game from the same shelf:
cap     asked whether it needs to explain itself (J/N, German for Y/N)
cap     and told no, it draws the board -- walls, the player and a
cap     target.
try     testibc; hinterhalt
run     testibc
wait    4
send    5\r
wait    4
send    Q
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     hinterhalt
wait    4
send    N\r
wait    6
snap
shot    i_am_i
cap     The Pascal quine printing its own source.
try     i_am_i
run     i_am_i
wait    5
shot    ident
for     liborder.os9
cap     liborder.os9 lists the object modules inside an OS-9 library and the
cap     order they are linked in.  version, mexist, modinfo and names each
cap     ask their own question about a module and have cards of their own.
try     liborder.os9 -?
run     liborder.os9 -?
wait    6
shot    keep
size    30 80
cap     keep takes a program off the collection onto your own disk, copying
cap     the binary and the files DOC/DEPENDS says it reads, laid out as the
cap     program expects them.  Here greg goes to a scratch directory
cap     standing in for your disk on /h1; keep names each file it wrote and
cap     records it so unkeep can take it back.
try     keep greg
run     export KEEP_TO=/dd/tmp/SYSB/kp; rm -rf /dd/tmp/SYSB/kp > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/SYSB > /nil 2>/nil
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     keep greg
wait    6
shot    kept
cap     kept lists what keep has written to your disk and what it came to.
cap     A program is kept first, then listed: each file, its size, and the
cap     total.
try     kept
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/SYSB > /nil 2>/nil; export KEEP_TO=/dd/tmp/SYSB/kp; rm -rf /dd/tmp/SYSB/kp > /nil 2>/nil; keep greg > /nil
wait    5
run     clear
wait    1
run     kept
wait    5
shot    launch
cap     launch's syntax and version.
try     launch -?
run     launch -?
wait    6
shot    makecrc
cap     makecrc generates C source for six CRC polynomials, one file each,
cap     and says nothing -- the files it leaves are the output.  Run in a
cap     writable directory, then listed.
try     makecrc; ls
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/UTILA/mc > /nil 2>/nil
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/mc > /nil 2>/nil
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/UTILA/mc
run     clear
wait    1
run     makecrc
wait    4
run     ls
wait    3
shot    map
cap     map shows the blocks a file occupies -- not a memory map, whatever
cap     the name suggests.
try     map -e DOC/INDEX
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     map -e DOC/INDEX
wait    5
shot    modinfo
cap     An OS-9 module header read back: name, type, size, edition and CRC.
cap     Every program on this disk is a module like this one.
try     modinfo CMDS/today
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     modinfo CMDS/today
wait    3
shot    names
cap     names is meant to list the modules inside a file.  Pointed at
cap     os9lib, the Fortran runtime, which holds many, it instead prints
cap     what looks like a stray program's own data and keeps prompting
cap     for input that never stops arriving, until it is stopped by
cap     hand.  modinfo answers the same question about one module at a
cap     time, reliably, and has its own card.
try     names CMDS/os9lib
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     names CMDS/os9lib
wait    5
kill
shot    ob68kdemo
cap     OmniBasic 1.16, a BASIC compiler, in its demonstration build.
try     ob68kdemo
run     ob68kdemo
wait    8
shot    oskversion
cap     oskversion prints the OS-9 version the system reports -- the number,
cap     the CPU it thinks it is on, and the edition.
try     oskversion
run     oskversion
wait    3
shot    passwd
cap     passwd changes a login password in SYS/password.  It matches on the
cap     user name, and the file it edits is plain text you can read here,
cap     shown with passwd's command format.
try     cat SYS/password; passwd -?
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat SYS/password
wait    5
run     passwd -?
wait    6
shot    phone
size    24 80
cap     `phone' connects two terminals over a communication path, so you
cap     can type to somebody on another terminal.  Give it one -- `phone
cap     /t1' -- and it draws its face and starts ringing, naming itself
cap     and the terminal it is calling, with a count of tries.  Control-E
cap     leaves.  With no path it prints its syntax.  The tail of the
cap     bottom line is the echoed command that was on that row when phone
cap     painted over it.
try     phone /t1
run     phone /t1
wait    28
shot    scsiutil
cap     scsiutil talks to a SCSI device.  With none to work on here it
cap     answers with its usage.
try     scsiutil -?
run     scsiutil -?
wait    8

shot    sddemo
cap     White's Speedisk 2.10, a disk defragmenter, drawing the map of a
cap     volume it is about to work on.
try     sddemo
run     sddemo
wait    10
shot    shdev
cap     shdev lists the system's device table -- what is mounted and the
cap     driver behind each one.  It prints its heading and no rows: there is
cap     no OS-9 device table here for it to walk.  dinfo asks a named device
cap     directly and does answer; it has its own card.
try     shdev
run     shdev
wait    6
shot    spline
cap     A B-spline surface demonstration: it draws a fixed 5x5 mesh and the
cap     surface fitted through it in Tektronix graphics codes, which a
cap     character terminal shows as text.  It reads nothing -- the stream is
cap     the same whatever it is given -- and at the end waits for a key.
try     spline | head -n 14
os9     spline !head -n 14
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     spline | head -n 14
wait    5
shot    sqrtx
cap     A square-root demonstration -- one of the small programs the forums
cap     circulated to prove a compiler worked.
try     sqrtx
run     sqrtx
wait    6
shot    ssl
cap     ssl asks RBF for a file's segment list -- the sectors it was
cap     given, in order.  It prints the heading and no rows for every
cap     file tried here, the segment information it wants not being
cap     available in this environment.  `map -e' answers the same
cap     question about a file's blocks, and does work; it has its own
cap     card.
try     ssl DOC/INDEX
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     ssl DOC/INDEX
wait    4
shot    top
cap     top lists the processes by their share of the CPU and refreshes every
cap     few seconds -- here `-a', which lists them all rather than only those
cap     that have used any CPU since the last pass.  The interval is an
cap     argument; interrupt it to leave.
cap     It used to print these headings and then take a bus error, which its
cap     card showed.  The cause was its own: it walked the process table by a
cap     count without bounding it by the array that holds it, ran off the end
cap     into whatever was beyond, and handed those bytes to F$GPrDsc as a
cap     process id.  Rebuilt from SRC/eff_top with the walk bounded,
cap     2026-09-18; the build needs no Microware module, so the star is gone
cap     from its entry too.
try     top -a 3
run     clear
wait    1
run     top -a 3
wait    12
snap
kill

shot    UAC_view
size    24 80
cap     The session directory a UAC system monitor kept on a 68040 VMEbus
cap     machine in January 1996: when each session ran, how many processes
cap     it started and how many errors it logged.  The highlighted one opens
cap     its process pages; Q quits.  Run it in the UAC directory, with
cap     PORT naming your terminal.
try     PORT=/term UAC_view -w=DATA
os9     setenv PORT /term; UAC_view -w=DATA
run     cd UAC
run     clear
run     PORT=/term UAC_view -w=DATA
wait    6
snap
quit    Q

shot    dmode
cap     The parameters of the RAM disk's device descriptor, r0, loaded
cap     from your own OS-9 -- a disk's descriptor has to be resident for
cap     dmode to find it.  Hex fields are decoded: `ctl' here is no
cap     format, multi-sector I/O and autosize.  `key=n' after the device
cap     changes a field in memory.
try     dmode /r0
run     load /h1/CMDS/BOOTOBJS/r0
run     clear
run     dmode /r0
wait    3
shot    tty
for     tty vecho lmargin
cap     tty names the terminal; vecho writes without a newline; lmargin
cap     sets a left margin on what follows.
try     tty; vecho "no newline here -- "; lmargin -?
run     tty
wait    5
run     vecho "no newline here -- "
wait    5
run     lmargin -?
wait    6
shot    ub68kdemo
cap     UniBasic 1.10, a BASIC compiler, in its demonstration build for the
cap     68000 -- its opening banner, which names the processor it was built
cap     for.  The symbol table is capped in the demo; nothing else is.
try     ub68kdemo | head -n 3
os9     ub68kdemo !head -n 3
run     ub68kdemo | head -n 3
wait    8
shot    ub68020demo
cap     The same UniBasic 1.10 demonstration built for the 68020 -- the one
cap     line that differs is the processor it announces.
try     ub68020demo | head -n 3
os9     ub68020demo !head -n 3
run     ub68020demo | head -n 3
wait    8
shot    vc
for     vc
cap     vc open: its welcome line and the empty grid.
try     vc
run     vc
wait    8
quit    q\r
shot    version
cap     version prints its own version and nothing else, whatever module it
cap     is named: asked about today, it answers for itself.  modinfo shows a
cap     module's edition.
try     version CMDS/today
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     version CMDS/today
wait    4
shot    yagi
cap     yagi designs a Yagi antenna to DL6WU's method: give it a frequency,
cap     an element count, a boom diameter and a tube size, and it works out
cap     the lengths and spacings.  144 MHz, nine elements, 20 mm boom.
try     yagi
run     yagi
wait    4
send    144\r
wait    3
send    9\r
wait    4
send    20\r
wait    4
send    N\r
wait    5
send    4\r
wait    10
shot    modules
size    32 80
for     cio csl csl020 math math881 os9lib rtfdat
cap     The runtime modules the starred programs need.  They are not
cap     programs -- you cannot run them -- so this is what they look like to
cap     `modinfo': name, type, edition and CRC. cio and math ship here by
cap     Microware's permission; SOURCES.txt records the exchange.  cio is
cap     shown whole, and for the other six the module name and edition.
try     modinfo CMDS/cio | head -n 6; modinfo CMDS/csl | grep "Module name\|Edition"; modinfo CMDS/csl020 | grep "Module name\|Edition"; modinfo CMDS/math | grep "Module name\|Edition"; modinfo CMDS/math881 | grep "Module name\|Edition"; modinfo CMDS/os9lib | grep "Module name\|Edition"; modinfo CMDS/rtfdat | grep "Module name\|Edition"
os9     modinfo CMDS/cio !head -n 6; modinfo CMDS/csl !grep "Module name\|Edition"; modinfo CMDS/csl020 !grep "Module name\|Edition"; modinfo CMDS/math !grep "Module name\|Edition"; modinfo CMDS/math881 !grep "Module name\|Edition"; modinfo CMDS/os9lib !grep "Module name\|Edition"; modinfo CMDS/rtfdat !grep "Module name\|Edition"
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     modinfo CMDS/cio | head -n 6
wait    12
run     modinfo CMDS/csl | grep "Module name\|Edition"
wait    12
run     modinfo CMDS/csl020 | grep "Module name\|Edition"
wait    12
run     modinfo CMDS/math | grep "Module name\|Edition"
wait    12
run     modinfo CMDS/math881 | grep "Module name\|Edition"
wait    12
run     modinfo CMDS/os9lib | grep "Module name\|Edition"
wait    12
run     modinfo CMDS/rtfdat | grep "Module name\|Edition"
wait    12
shot    drivers
for     keydrv.mm1 msdrv.901_340 msdrv_340.901.ms rb37c65 scsi_mm1a snddrv windio.52
cap     The MM/1 drivers -- keyboard, mouse, floppy, SCSI, sound and the
cap     windowing terminal.  They are device drivers for hardware that is
cap     not here, so `modinfo' is what there is to show: type $C (device
cap     driver) rather than $1 (program).  Each is asked for, one line apiece.
try     modinfo CMDS/MM1/keydrv.mm1 | head -n 4; modinfo CMDS/MM1/msdrv.901_340 | head -n 4; modinfo CMDS/MM1/rb37c65 | head -n 4; modinfo CMDS/MM1/scsi_mm1a | head -n 4; modinfo CMDS/MM1/snddrv | head -n 4; modinfo CMDS/MM1/windio.52 | head -n 4; modinfo CMDS/MM1/msdrv_340.901.ms | head -n 4
os9     modinfo CMDS/MM1/keydrv.mm1 !head -n 4; modinfo CMDS/MM1/msdrv.901_340 !head -n 4; modinfo CMDS/MM1/rb37c65 !head -n 4; modinfo CMDS/MM1/scsi_mm1a !head -n 4; modinfo CMDS/MM1/snddrv !head -n 4; modinfo CMDS/MM1/windio.52 !head -n 4; modinfo CMDS/MM1/msdrv_340.901.ms !head -n 4
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     modinfo CMDS/MM1/keydrv.mm1 | head -n 4
wait    12
run     modinfo CMDS/MM1/msdrv.901_340 | head -n 4
wait    12
run     modinfo CMDS/MM1/rb37c65 | head -n 4
wait    12
run     modinfo CMDS/MM1/scsi_mm1a | head -n 4
wait    12
run     modinfo CMDS/MM1/snddrv | head -n 4
wait    12
run     modinfo CMDS/MM1/windio.52 | head -n 4
wait    12
run     modinfo CMDS/MM1/msdrv_340.901.ms | head -n 4
wait    12
shot    modules2
for     ptxm wn.stb
cap     Two more modules that are not programs: `ptxm', Nick Holgate's Path
cap     Table eXtension Module, which `ptxminst' installs, and `wn.stb', the
cap     symbol table that ships beside the WN web server.
try     modinfo CMDS/ptxm | head -n 12; modinfo CMDS/WN/wn.stb | head -n 4
os9     modinfo CMDS/ptxm !head -n 12; modinfo CMDS/WN/wn.stb !head -n 4
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     modinfo CMDS/ptxm | head -n 12
wait    12
run     modinfo CMDS/WN/wn.stb | head -n 4
wait    10
shot    mtst
for     mtst
cap     mtst exercises the C maths library and prints what each function
cap     returned beside what it was given -- ceil, floor and round on the
cap     same number, integer and floating side by side.  It is one of the
cap     small programs a port was checked with.
try     mtst | head -n 16
os9     mtst !head -n 16
run     mtst | head -n 16
wait    12
shot    fontgen
for     fontgen
cap     fontgen writes the source of a character font: an OS-9 data
cap     module in assembler, psect and all, ready for the 80-column card
cap     it was written for.  It reads its bitmap from standard input and
cap     will sit waiting on the terminal if you forget to give it one.
try     fontgen < /nil | head -n 10
os9     fontgen </nil !head -n 10
run     fontgen < /nil | head -n 10
wait    12
shot    dpark
cap     dpark restores an RBF disk's head to track 00 -- what you did before
cap     moving a drive.  With no disk head to park here it answers with its
cap     command format.
try     dpark -?
run     dpark -?
wait    5
shot    bsplt68
cap     Takes an OS9Boot file apart into the modules inside it, writing
cap     each one out under its own module name.  A boot file is simply
cap     modules end to end, so one is made here with cat and split
cap     straight back into the two that went in.
try     bsplt68 OS9Boot
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/BSPL > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/BSPL
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/BSPL
wait    1
run     cat /dd/CMDS/cat /dd/CMDS/clear > OS9Boot
wait    4
run     clear
wait    1
run     ls -l
wait    3
run     bsplt68 OS9Boot
wait    6
run     ls -l
wait    4
shot    mexist
cap     mexist tests whether a module is in memory and sets its exit
cap     status -- zero when the module is there, one when it is not.
cap     bash is resident; nosuchmodule is not.
try     mexist bash; echo "status $?"; mexist nosuchmodule; echo "status $?"
run     mexist bash; echo "status $?"
wait    4
run     mexist nosuchmodule; echo "status $?"
wait    4
shot    time
size    24 80
cap     Times a program: it runs the command you give it, lets that command
cap     print whatever it prints, and then adds how long it took -- real
cap     time, and the processor time spent in the program and in the system.
try     time wc DOC/samples/jabber.txt
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     time wc DOC/samples/jabber.txt
wait    5

shot    vis
size    24 80
cap     vis runs a command over and over and redraws the screen with its
cap     output each time, the way `watch' does elsewhere.  Its header names
cap     the command, the delay between runs in seconds and the number of
cap     runs so far; here `today', once a second.  Control-C stops it.
try     vis -d1 today
run     clear
wait    1
run     vis -d1 today
wait    6
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    repeat
size    24 80
cap     repeat runs a command a number of times -- `repeat 2 today' runs
cap     today twice, one after the other.  It hands the line to $SHELL,
cap     which SYS/login sets to ksh.
try     repeat 2 today
run     repeat 2 today
wait    4
shot    cron
cap     A scheduling daemon: it reads usr/lib/crontab -- shown first --
cap     and hands each command to Microware's shell at its minute, hour
cap     and day, running until it is stopped.  Started in the background
cap     here, bash prints the id it got, and `signal' answers status 0
cap     because there was a daemon there to take it.  Use the id you see
cap     rather than the 3 shown.
try     cron &
run     set +H
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat usr/lib/crontab
wait    5
run     cron &
wait    4
run     signal 3 0; echo "status $?"
wait    4
shot    setime
super
cap     setime prompts for the date and time as one string, two digits
cap     each: year, month, day, hour, minute, second, in that order, so
cap     the format is what this card has to give you.  Nothing here can
cap     move the clock, and it is not this program's doing: under os9exec
cap     the call that sets the time does nothing and reports success, so
cap     `setime', `setime2', `setimex' and `setyear' all report the time
cap     and leave it alone.  On your own system they set it.
try     setime -?
run     setime -?
wait    5
shot    adduser
super
size    30 80
cap     adduser adds a login to the system, or removes one with -r.  With no
cap     name given it prints its usage: the options and the command forms it
cap     accepts.
try     adduser -?
run     adduser -?
wait    6
shot    combine
cap     combine interleaves two files byte by byte -- the even bytes from
cap     one, the odd bytes from the other -- which is how a 16-bit EPROM
cap     image is rebuilt from its two 8-bit halves.  Eight A's and eight
cap     B's go in; the interleaved result comes out.  The file it makes
cap     has no permissions set: anyone but the super-user gives it some
cap     with attr before reading it.
try     combine tmp/UTILA/cmb/even tmp/UTILA/cmb/odd tmp/UTILA/cmb/mix; cat tmp/UTILA/cmb/mix; echo
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/UTILA/cmb > /nil 2>/nil
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/cmb > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf 'AAAAAAAA' > /dd/tmp/UTILA/cmb/even
run     printf 'BBBBBBBB' > /dd/tmp/UTILA/cmb/odd
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     combine tmp/UTILA/cmb/even tmp/UTILA/cmb/odd tmp/UTILA/cmb/mix
wait    3
run     cat tmp/UTILA/cmb/mix; echo
wait    3
shot    demerge
cap     demerge splits a file of several OS-9 modules back into one file
cap     per module.  OS-9's `merge' is concatenation, so `cat' makes the
cap     input: today and tty joined into one file, then taken apart into
cap     today and tty again.
try     demerge both; ls -l
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/UTILA/dm > /nil 2>/nil
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/dm > /nil 2>/nil
run     cat /dd/CMDS/today /dd/CMDS/tty > /dd/tmp/UTILA/dm/both
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/UTILA/dm
run     clear
wait    1
run     demerge both
wait    4
run     ls -l
wait    3
shot    ptob
size    46 80
cap     ptob turns a file of bit patterns back into characters, the other
cap     half of `btop': `Hello' becomes rows of dots and O's, and ptob
cap     reads those back to `Hello'.
try     cat bits; ptob bits > back; cat back
os9     cat bits; ptob bits >back; cat back
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/pb > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf 'Hello' > /dd/tmp/UTILA/pb/t
run     btop /dd/tmp/UTILA/pb/t > /dd/tmp/UTILA/pb/bits
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/UTILA/pb
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat bits
wait    2
run     ptob bits > back
wait    2
run     cat back; echo
wait    2
shot    trunc
cap     trunc cuts a file to a given length: `trunc <path> <num>'.  A
cap     sixteen-byte file is cut to eight, and the listing either side of
cap     it is the whole demonstration.
try     ls -l f; trunc f 8; ls -l f; cat f; echo
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/tr > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf '0123456789ABCDEF' > /dd/tmp/UTILA/tr/f
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/UTILA/tr
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     ls -l f
wait    2
run     trunc f 8
wait    2
run     ls -l f; cat f; echo
wait    3
shot    clear
cap     clear clears the terminal screen.  A directory listing is printed
cap     and then wiped by clear -- only the line typed after it is left.
try     dir CMDS; clear; echo "the listing above was wiped by clear"
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     dir CMDS
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     echo "the listing above was wiped by clear"
wait    2
shot    lgrep
cap     lgrep lists the files a pattern appears in, names only: it runs
cap     `grep -l' and strips what grep adds, so the list can go straight
cap     onto another command line.  The grep it runs is your own OS-9's,
cap     loaded from /h1 -- this disk's GNU grep answers differently and
cap     lgrep prints nothing from it.  DOC/README-GREP compares the searchers.
try     lgrep ksh SYS/password SYS/login SYS/motd
run     load /h1/CMDS/grep
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     lgrep ksh SYS/password SYS/login SYS/motd
wait    4
shot    suse
cap     A CPU benchmark in 190 bytes of assembler: the Sieve of
cap     Eratosthenes run a thousand times, then it exits.  It prints
cap     nothing, so the time it takes is its result.
try     time suse
run     time suse
wait    8
shot    pri
cap     pri changes a process's priority: `pri <pid> <priority>'.  It
cap     prints nothing whatever happens, so the exit status is all there
cap     is to see -- and here that is 221 for every invocation tried,
cap     including a live process and no arguments at all.  221 is
cap     module-not-found, which is not an answer about a process; what it
cap     is looking for has not been established, and there is no source
cap     for it on the disk to read.
try     pri 2 128; echo "status $?"
run     pri 2 128; echo "status $?"
wait    3
run     pri; echo "status $?"
wait    3
shot    snd_sig
cap     Sends a signal to one or more processes named by id.  With no
cap     option it sends the wake signal and says nothing; `-(n)' sends
cap     signal n, and 2 is the keyboard abort, which ends a process.
cap     `cron' is started in the background here and bash prints the id
cap     it got -- use the id you see rather than the 3 shown.  Woken, then
cap     asked for an id nothing holds, which is how you know it found the
cap     first one, and then sent the abort.
try     snd_sig 3
run     set +H
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     cron &
wait    4
run     snd_sig 3
wait    3
run     snd_sig 99
wait    3
run     snd_sig -2 3; echo "status $?"
wait    4
shot    setime2
super
cap     setime2 sets the system date and time from the fields
cap     `yyy mm dd hh mm ss' at its prompt -- yyy counts years from 1900,
cap     so 126 is 2026 -- and prints the date.  os9exec does not move the
cap     clock, so what it prints is today's date, not the one typed.
try     setime2
run     clear
wait    1
run     setime2
wait    2
send    126 9 3 14 30 0\r
wait    3
shot    setyear
cap     setyear sets the year, and only the year, leaving the month, day
cap     and time alone: `setyear 2026'.  It prints the date it ends up
cap     with, which is what the card shows -- under os9exec the call that
cap     sets the time does nothing and reports success, so the date it
cap     prints is the one it started from.
try     setyear 2026
run     setyear 2026
wait    3
shot    suspend
cap     suspend removes a process from the system, as its own Function
cap     line says, rather than pausing it; `-s' reaches system programs
cap     too.  Its syntax, there being no process here to remove.
try     suspend
run     suspend
wait    3
shot    umusek
cap     umusek is a music editor.  It wants a hardware screen address and
cap     stops without one: `DS_ScAdd Error 208', then `Can't get screen
cap     addr, bye'.
try     umusek; echo "status $?"
run     umusek; echo "status $?"
wait    5
shot    unpacklib.os9
cap     unpacklib extracts the object modules from an OS-9 library into
cap     separate .r files.  Run on the C library it writes them into the
cap     current directory, stopping at the first record that is not a
cap     module.
try     unpacklib.os9 alib.l; ls
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/UTILA/ul > /nil 2>/nil
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/ul > /nil 2>/nil
run     cp /dd/LIB/alib.l /dd/tmp/UTILA/ul/alib.l
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/UTILA/ul
run     clear
wait    1
run     unpacklib.os9 alib.l
wait    5
run     ls
wait    3
shot    ynad
size    24 80
cap     YNAD -- Yet Another Name & Address program, a full-screen contact
cap     database.  Its title bar on opening; there is no address file yet.
try     ynad
run     ynad
wait    8
snap
send    q
wait    2
shot    bootlogger
cap     Writes the time the machine came up into SYS/bootlog and says
cap     nothing.  It returns silently whenever you run it, so the log is
cap     the only way to see that it did anything -- and running it by hand
cap     stamps the log with the time you ran it rather than a boot.
try     bootlogger; cat SYS/bootlog
run     rm -f /dd/SYS/bootlog > /nil 2>/nil
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     bootlogger; echo "status $?"
wait    4
run     cat SYS/bootlog
wait    3
shot    screen_nocio
cap     screen_nocio is the trap-free build of `screen', a login greeting:
cap     it picks a file at random from $HOME/.SCREENS and runs it through
cap     `shell' -- your own OS-9's, which SYS/login loads from /h1.  Here
cap     the one file holds `today', so the greeting is the date in words.
cap     The build carries a one-line fix, SRC/screen/README.OSK.
try     screen_nocio
run     load /h1/CMDS/shell
wait    3
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/UTILA/home > /nil 2>/nil
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/home/.SCREENS > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf 'today\r' > /dd/tmp/UTILA/home/.SCREENS/hi
run     export HOME=/dd/tmp/UTILA/home
run     clear
wait    1
run     screen_nocio
wait    6

shot    transfer
cap     transfer copies files from GDOS disks to OS-9 and takes no
cap     options -- not a general device-to-device copier; `cp' and
cap     `dsave' are those.  It wants the GDOS device DGDOS0, which is not
cap     present.
try     transfer
run     transfer
wait    4
send    \r
wait    2
shot    preset
cap     preset programs the terminal's function keys with a fixed set of
cap     definitions -- dir, umacs, r68, l68, dsave and so on.  It takes no
cap     arguments and ignores any given.
try     preset
run     preset
wait    4
shot    vlen
cap     vlen is a variable-length-record demonstration: it builds a small
cap     filesystem of its own, writes a hundred records of differing
cap     length, and reads them back deleting some as it goes.  The tail of
cap     the run, ending `vlTest completed successfully'.
try     vlen > vl.out; tail -l=16 vl.out
os9     vlen >vl.out; tail -l=16 vl.out
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/UTILA/vl > /nil 2>/nil
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/vl > /nil 2>/nil
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/UTILA/vl
run     clear
wait    1
run     vlen > vl.out
wait    14
run     tail -l=16 vl.out
wait    4
shot    mvolformat
cap     mvolformat formats a set of floppies with sequential volume
cap     numbers.  With no drive here, its syntax and options are what
cap     there is to show.
try     mvolformat
run     mvolformat
wait    3
shot    rndir
cap     rndir converts the names of the subdirectories in a directory to
cap     upper or lower case; `-l' makes them lower case, `-q' works
cap     quietly.  Its options.
try     rndir -?
run     rndir -?
wait    4

shot    freeb
size    14 80
cap     Lists the free space on a disk block by block, rather than as one
cap     total: the volume name and when it was made, the size of the disk,
cap     then every free block with where it starts and how many sectors it
cap     holds.  -t counts them by size instead, and -h leaves the header out.
try     freeb; freeb -t
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     freeb
wait    3
run     freeb -t
wait    4

shot    os9dsk
size    16 80
cap     Reads a Color Computer .DSK disk image -- the format a CoCo emulator
cap     keeps a floppy in -- and lists the OS-9 disk inside it.  The image
cap     here is a public-domain disk from 1985, and reads as easily as a new
cap     one.  -get copies a file out of it; -proc prints a procedure file
cap     of makdir, chd and -get lines that extracts the whole tree.
try     os9dsk -dir PUBDOM19.DSK; os9dsk -dir PUBDOM19.DSK CMDS
run     builtin cd /dd/DOC/os9dsk
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     os9dsk -dir PUBDOM19.DSK
wait    4
run     os9dsk -dir PUBDOM19.DSK CMDS
wait    5

shot    rsdsk
size    16 80
cap     Reads the RS-DOS side of a CoCo .DSK image -- Disk Extended BASIC
cap     disks, where a name is eight characters and three and a file is
cap     BASIC, binary, data or text.  The image here holds a menu program,
cap     a music player and its tunes.
try     rsdsk -dir PUBDOM09.DSK
run     builtin cd /dd/DOC/rsdsk
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     rsdsk -dir PUBDOM09.DSK
wait    5

shot    zc
size    24 80
cap     A US postal zip code in, the city and state out.  The table it reads
cap     is three quarters of a megabyte of them, one line per code, and a
cap     code that is not in the table is told so rather than guessed at.
cap     With no argument it opens a small form instead, where C clears the
cap     field and Q leaves.
try     zc 60115
run     clear
wait    1
run     zc 60115
wait    3
run     zc 02139
wait    3
run     zc 99999
wait    3

shot    resize
size    30 100
cap     Asks the terminal how big its window is and prints LINES and COLUMNS,
cap     for less and the other programs that read them before the termcap's
cap     24 by 80.  This window is 30 by 100.  At the bash prompt SYS/login
cap     gives you, typing `resize' sets the two; -s prints them for your own
cap     OS-9's shell instead.  A terminal that does not answer gets a second,
cap     and then resize prints nothing.
try     resize
run     clear
wait    1
run     resize
wait    4
run     resize -s
wait    4
